WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter, Author,&nbsp;
and Instructor: Somebody has said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.480
the job of the artist is to see and&nbsp;
then to show others what they've seen.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.080
Film Courage: Can we talk about moments&nbsp;
that heal or repair a character?

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:20.480
Christopher: I love moments in film where there&nbsp;
is restoration of some kind that happens in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:31.360
way that seems believable, that doesn't seem&nbsp;
shangri-la. I think about the film Blood Diamond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.480
in this regard. There's really two&nbsp;
stories that happen in Blood Diamond.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:49.120
We have the story of a South African diamond&nbsp;
merchant played by Leonardo DiCaprio who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.360
trafficking in these blood diamonds (I think&nbsp;
this the movie takes place in Sierra Leone)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:02.720
and so he's on the hunt for this giant diamond&nbsp;
that he's heard has been found out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:12.160
mines that are run by the rebel military and the&nbsp;
rebel military conscripts boys as child soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:21.760
both to fight for them but also to work in these&nbsp;
mines. The other main character is played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:29.600
Djimon Hounsou and he's the dad of a boy who has&nbsp;
been abducted and taken away from his village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:38.240
made to be a soldier who commits atrocities&nbsp;
so this young boy 10 years old maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:46.880
has been made to do horrible things and his&nbsp;
dad is determined to find his son and somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.840
bring him back to the family and as he's&nbsp;
doing that he gets forced into working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:01.440
digging for diamonds for the people who stole his&nbsp;
son from him and he finds this massive diamond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:11.760
and he buries it out away from the&nbsp;
camp and continues looking for his son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.760
Leonardo DiCaprio finds out Djimon&nbsp;
Hounsou's got this massive diamond hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:27.520
and at gunpoint takes him to dig up&nbsp;
this diamond and just as they're digging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:35.840
for this diamond chime and Hounsou's son&nbsp;
shows up and is holding a gun on both men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:44.800
Leonardo DiCaprio alerts Djimon Hounsou's who's&nbsp;
digging for the diamond that his son is here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:55.360
and so this father turns to his son who now turns&nbsp;
the gun to him and is calling out the sons calling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:03.840
out for help and Djimon Hounsou's says you're&nbsp;
not one of them you're my son and i'm your father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.360
your mother waits for you your sister waits&nbsp;
for you the wild dog who listens to no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:18.800
waits for you at home you're not a bad boy I&nbsp;
know they made you do bad things but you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:27.280
going to come home to be me and be my son again&nbsp;
and I’m going to love you and in the midst of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:36.720
the father is walking toward his son walking&nbsp;
toward the barrel of the gun and the son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.880
begins to cry and slowly lowers the gun and&nbsp;
allows himself to be embraced by his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.240
that is written directed and acted&nbsp;
in a way that you believe that

00:03:52.640 --> 00:04:00.480
that is a moment of restoration and recovery&nbsp;
that maybe shouldn't even be possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.080
that it would be that you could recover a child&nbsp;
who has been put through those experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:16.160
the film presents it with a kind&nbsp;
of emotional believability that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:23.920
certainly represents a defining&nbsp;
moment for that boy and his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.200
that is going to be the pivot point&nbsp;
certainly this kid's going to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:39.440
probably lots of therapy and lots of love&nbsp;
over lots of years to completely emerge from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:46.720
that awful darkness but in that moment you&nbsp;
believe that there is real hope and that

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:56.960
this moment represents a before and after that&nbsp;
they will look back 10 years from now and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:06.160
that is the moment that the healing began that the&nbsp;
restoration began and that love began to eclipse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:17.360
hate in the life of this boy I think healing's&nbsp;
like that it doesn't usually come in a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:26.880
usually it comes a little bit at a time but it it&nbsp;
has a beginning and as storytellers as filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:37.280
we have the capacity to aim the camera aim the&nbsp;
attention of the audience at even the minutest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:46.880
movements so that we are able to perceive&nbsp;
the moment when a process of healing begins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:54.160
and actually allow the audience to to&nbsp;
see feel and appreciate what's happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.800
in a way that if we were on stage&nbsp;
and we were looking at the proscenium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:07.920
like you'd miss it you'd never know what was&nbsp;
happening we we have the capacity to to zoom in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:16.720
on these massively significant movements&nbsp;
of character that are almost imperceptible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.800
somebody has said that the job of the artist is&nbsp;
to see and then to show others what they've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:33.840
and for me this is an example of that&nbsp;
of looking closely enough to see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:43.600
monumental thing that's happening in this tiny&nbsp;
little frame and blow that up so that we see it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:50.960
we understand it we we appreciate its meaning&nbsp;
and we feel it and it seems that combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:57.040
meaning and emotion are another of the things&nbsp;
that we we just really crave from our stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:05.680
just out of curiosity what was the Leonardo&nbsp;
DiCaprio character doing during that moment he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:12.960
he was standing and watching he&nbsp;
had he had his assault rifle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:21.520
I think he was ready to shoot the boy&nbsp;
if he felt he needed to but he was he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.560
was also witness he was bearing&nbsp;
witness to this miracle moment

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.800
so even I haven't seen the film but because&nbsp;
his intentions sounds like they were not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.160
honorable either okay so he but he's seeing&nbsp;
this and do you see a little bit of change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:47.600
in that character at all yes yeah after that he&nbsp;
he discovers that actually he's been shot in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:56.000
fire fight that happened just before this scene&nbsp;
he ends up really sacrificing himself to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:07.280
father and son escape so yeah it does actually&nbsp;
